<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Peregonovka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Perehonivka · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Peregonovka was fought in September 1919 near the village of Peregonovka (Perehonivka), near Uman in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>'s Revolutionary Insurrectionary Army, cornered by White forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-of-south-russia">Armed Forces of South Russia</a>, turned and routed them. The insurgents then raided deep behind White lines, disrupting supplies for Denikin's advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In mid-June 1919 Andrei Shkuro, commander of the Kuban Cossacks, exploited the discord between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and the Ukrainian anarchists to raid the anarchist capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a>, forcing the Insurgent Army to retreat west towards right-bank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Near Kherson the insurgents allied with the otaman Nykyfor Hryhoriv, then assassinated him for his ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White movement</a> and his part in antisemitic pogroms. Strengthened by defectors from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, they began attacking behind the White lines.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> commander Yakov Slashchov struck back, and the insurgents retreated for two weeks towards Uman, carrying 8,000 wounded and sick and skirmishing with the Whites daily. The Ukrainian People's Republic, which held Uman, offered neutral ground where their wounded could be treated. Dug in at the village of Peregonovka, outnumbered, surrounded and poorly equipped, the insurgents decided they had retreated far enough.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Whites attacked on 22 September across hilly ground cut by deep ravines between the Syniukha and Iatran rivers. Over the next days they drove the insurgents from the high ground, but poor coordination left their flanks exposed. The next day a White column occupied Uman, opening a wide gap that left the Simferopol Officer Regiment isolated at Rogovo. Nestor Makhno regrouped opposite its weakened flank and told his exhausted troops that their 600-kilometre retreat was over.</p><p>After a feint towards Krutenkoe that evening, the insurgents attacked at 03:00. The Whites at first pushed them back to the outskirts of Peregonovka, but Makhno led his sotnia in a flanking charge with sabres that forced the Whites into retreat and then a rout. Fleeing towards the Syniukha, some White units drowned crossing it under fire at Burakivka; the colonel Vladimir Almendinger reached Novoukrainka with barely 100 men left of six companies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The insurgents split up to seize territory, and within little more than a week held Kryvyi Rih, Nikopol, Melitopol, Mariupol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a> among other cities, taking Katerynoslav by 20 October and cutting the supply lines of the Whites. The White advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> halted about 200 kilometres from the capital, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> of Konstantin Mamontov and Shkuro were sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Historians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> have linked the victory to the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a>'s forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Peregonovka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The insurgents fought Germans, nationalists and Bolsheviks, and in September 1919 their victory at <b>Peregonovka</b> disrupted Denikin's march on Moscow.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-territory">Free Territory</a></li></ul></section>
